Turn on the memory device, so a RAM disk can be the root device.

The kernel now boots all the way through bsd_init and stops where it should:

    Waiting on <dict><key>IOProviderClass</key><string>IOMedia</string>
               <key>Content</key><string>Apple_HFS</string></dict>
    Still waiting for root device

Nothing publishes an IOMedia, because nothing drives this board's storage.
The way out is the memory device: bsd/dev/memdev.c turns a region of memory
named on the command line into a block device, which is exactly the shape of
what the shim already does -- it places the kernel, the device tree and the
boot arguments in physical memory and could as easily place a disk image.

Two things stand between that and here.

First, the whole body of mdevinit() is inside

    #ifdef CONFIG_MEMDEV_INSECURE

and nothing in xnu-4903 defines that symbol -- not config/MASTER, not
config/MASTER.arm64, not makedefs/MakeInc.def. The function compiles to
nothing. The name is Apple's own warning: the boot arguments can point the
device at any physical address, so a kernel built this way will hand out
whatever is there. That is a real objection to shipping it and no objection
at all to a bring-up kernel that has no security model yet and whose whole
boot chain is already a custom shim.

Second, and less obvious: mdevinit() is never called. Across the entire tree
there are exactly three mentions of the name -- its prototype in memdev.c,
its definition in memdev.c, and a stale prototype in bsd/dev/memdev.h that
has a different signature and is included by nothing. So defining the macro
alone would still leave the parser unreached.

The call goes into bsd_init() right after inittodr(), which is after
vfsinit() -- mdevadd() makes devfs nodes -- and immediately before the
"Mount the root file system" loop, which is where setconf() runs
IOFindBSDRoot(). It sits outside that loop deliberately: the loop retries,
and a second mdevinit() would panic in mdevadd() on a device it had already
added.

The boot arguments mdevinit() looks for are

    pmd<N>=<base>.<size>     a device in physical memory
    vmd<N>=<base>.<size>     a device in the kernel's virtual memory

with N a hex digit, base and size hex and both page aligned. pmd is the one
that matches what the shim can do. Worth knowing: it scans only the first 256
bytes of the command line, so the memory device argument belongs near the
front of a long one.

With this in, a root RAM disk needs three more things, none of them here:
the image has to be placed and its address passed (the shim), rd=md0 has to
name it as the root device (a boot argument), and the kernel has to be able
to mount what is in it -- which means HFS, which Apple moved out of xnu after
10.12 and which this tree therefore does not have.

--- a/bsd/conf/Makefile.template
+++ b/bsd/conf/Makefile.template
@@ -80,6 +80,11 @@
 ubc_subr.o_CFLAGS_ADD			+= -Wshorten-64-to-32
 vnode_pager.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -Wshorten-64-to-32
 vm_unix.o_CFLAGS_ADD			+= -Wshorten-64-to-32
+
+# The memory device is compiled out unless CONFIG_MEMDEV_INSECURE is defined,
+# and nothing in the tree defines it. See the patch header.
+memdev.o_CFLAGS_ADD			+= -DCONFIG_MEMDEV_INSECURE=1
+
 pthread_synch.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-missing-prototypes
 pthread_support.o_CFLAGS_ADD		+= -Wno-unused-parameter -Wno-missing-prototypes
 
--- a/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
+++ b/bsd/kern/bsd_init.c
@@ -387,6 +387,12 @@
 extern thread_t cloneproc(task_t, coalition_t, proc_t, int, int);
 extern int      (*mountroot)(void);
 
+/*
+ * bsd/dev/memdev.c. Declared here because bsd/dev/memdev.h carries a stale
+ * prototype with a different signature and is included by nothing.
+ */
+extern void     mdevinit(int the_cnt);
+
 lck_grp_t * proc_lck_grp;
 lck_grp_t * proc_slock_grp;
 lck_grp_t * proc_fdmlock_grp;
@@ -922,6 +928,16 @@
 	bsd_init_kprintf("calling inittodr\n");
 	inittodr(0);
 
+	/*
+	 * Publish any memory devices named by the boot arguments, so that
+	 * setconf() below can find one when "rd=md0" asks for it. This has to
+	 * happen after vfsinit(), because mdevadd() makes devfs nodes, and
+	 * outside the retry loop, because a second call would panic on a device
+	 * it had already added.
+	 */
+	bsd_init_kprintf("calling mdevinit\n");
+	mdevinit(0);
+
 	/* Mount the root file system. */
 	while (TRUE) {
 		int err;
